title="Steven Schaefer">Steven Schaefer</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><fr:p><fr:em>Deterministic regular expressions</fr:em> are a grammar formalism for defining regular expressions that correspond to deterministic regular languages.

In the spirit of <fr:link
type="local"
href="bruggemannkleinwood.xml"
addr="bruggemannkleinwood"
title="Deterministic regular languages">Bruggemann-Klein and Wood</fr:link> and <fr:link
type="local"
href="krishnaswami_typed_2019.xml"
addr="krishnaswami_typed_2019"
title="A typed, algebraic approach to parsing">Krishaswami and Yallop</fr:link>, inductively define the deterministic regular expressions in terms of their <fr:link
type="local"
href="sss-0014.xml"
addr="sss-0014"
title="Nullable Grammar">nullability</fr:link>, <fr:link
type="local"
href="sss-000Y.xml"
addr="sss-000Y"
title="First Set">first set</fr:link>, and <fr:link
type="local"
href="sss-0010.xml"
addr="sss-0010"
title="FollowLast Set">followlast set</fr:link>:
  <fr:ul><fr:li><fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[\epsilon ]]></fr:tex>, the grammar accepting only the empty string.</fr:li>
    <fr:li><fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[\texttt {"}c\texttt {"}]]></fr:tex>, the grammar only accepting the length 1 string comprising the character <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex> for some character <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex> drawn from the alphabet</fr:li>
    <fr:li><fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[r \otimes  r']]></fr:tex>, the concatenation of <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[r]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[r']]></fr:tex> where <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[\neg  \mathsf {Null}(r)]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(r) \cap  \mathsf {First}(r') = \emptyset ]]></fr:tex></fr:li>
    <fr:li><fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[r \oplus  r']]></fr:tex>, the disjunction of <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[r]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[r']]></fr:tex> where <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[\neg  (\mathsf {Null}(r) \land  \mathsf {Null}(r'))]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {First}(r) \cap  \mathsf {First}(r') = \emptyset ]]></fr:tex></fr:li>
    <fr:li><fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[{r}^{*}]]></fr:tex>, the Kleene star of <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[r]]></fr:tex> where <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[\neg  \mathsf {Null}(r)]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(r) \cap  \mathsf {First}(r) = \emptyset ]]></fr:tex></fr:li></fr:ul></fr:p><fr:p>In each of the above constructors, the constraints on <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {First}(r)]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex
display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathsf {FLast}(r)]]></fr:tex> remove sources of ambiguity in a regular expressions. They provide a sound guarantee that the resulting regular expression has at most one parse for any string.</fr:p></fr:mainmatter><fr:backmatter /></fr:tree><fr:tree
